{"patch":{"config":{"title":"Antranig Basman","baseURL":"https://ponder.org.uk","defaultContentLanguage":"en","mainSections":["post","posts"],"author":{"name":"Antranig Basman","website":"/","email":"amb26web@ponder.org.uk","twitter":"amb26ponder"},"params":{"homeTitle":"Antranig Basman","subtitle":"Building habitable technologies","bio":"img/20230209_135356 - bio - cropped.jpg","logo":"img/pond.jpg","favicon":"img/favicon.ico","dateFormat":"January 2, 2006","comments":true,"socialShare":true,"showRelatedPosts":true,"commentsLayer":"beautifulWend.comments.giscus","customCSS":["css/ponder.css"],"mediaIcons":[{"title":"Twitter profile","href":"http://twitter.com/amb26ponder","icon":"fab fa-twitter"},{"title":"Send me a message","href":"mailto:amb26webpage@ponder.org.uk","icon":"fa fa-envelope"},{"title":"Antranig's GitHub profile","href":"http://github.com/amb26","icon":"fab fa-github-alt"},{"title":"My papers on DBLP","href":"https://dblp.org/pid/68/5013.html","icon":"fa fa-university"},{"title":"My LinkedIn profile","href":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/antranigbasman/","icon":"fab fa-linkedin"}],"giscus":{"repo":"amb26/ponder","repoId":"R_kgDOOKllGA","category":"Announcements","categoryId":"DIC_kwDOOKllGM4CvhAS"}},"menu":{"main":[{"name":"Posts","url":"post","weight":1},{"name":"WiP","url":"wip","weight":1},{"name":"Docs","identifier":"docs","url":"docs","weight":3},{"name":"Fluid Signals","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-signals","weight":1},{"name":"Fluid Integration Language","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-integration-language","weight":2},{"name":"Fluid View","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-view","weight":3},{"name":"Fluid PTF","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-ptf","weight":4},{"name":"Fluid tldraw integration","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-tldraw","weight":5},{"name":"Layer Linearisation","parent":"docs","url":"docs/linearisation-algorithm","weight":6},{"name":"About","url":"page/about/","weight":3},{"name":"Communities","url":"communities","weight":4},{"name":"Projects","url":"projects","weight":5},{"name":"Terms","url":"term","weight":5}]}},"features":{"selfHosted":true,"hljs":true}},"pages":[{"kind":"page","type":"term","section":"term","title":"Divergence","relPermalink":"/term/divergence/","permalink":"https://ponder.org.uk/term/divergence/","outputPath":"/term/divergence/index.html","params":{"title":"Divergence","categories":["term"]},"sourcePath":"content/term/divergence.md","content":"<p>Defined in my <a href=\"https://www.ppig.org/files/2016-PPIG-27th-Basman1.pdf\">2016 paper</a>, <strong>divergence</strong> represents\nany discrepancy between the runtime state of a program, and the state through which it can be authored. Typical\nsources of divergence include the program’s <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Call_stack\"><em>call stack</em></a>,\nuninterpreted addresses within the <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79923/what-and-where-are-the-stack-and-heap\"><em>heap</em></a>,\n<a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Core/Scripting/Events\">event listeners</a> and\ntypical sources of object representation such as <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtual_method_table\">vtables</a> and the like.</p>\n<p>In a tradition of materialised programming, divergence should be minimised, in order to reduce the mental burden on\nthose trying to visualise the effects of their edits.</p>\n<p>Closely related notions are the <strong>Gulf of Execution</strong>, and <strong>Gulf of Evaluation</strong>, originally coined by Don Norman in\nhis 1988 <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Design_of_Everyday_Things\">Design of Everyday Things</a>.</p>\n<p>Jonathan Edwards centres bridging these gulfs in his 2005 paper\n<a href=\"https://www.subtext-lang.org/OOPSLA05.pdf\">Subtext: Uncovering the Simplicity of Programming</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The Gulf of Execution is the difficulty\nof translating a desired goal into an action to be executed. The\nGulf of Evaluation is the difficulty of determining whether an\nobservable state meets the desired goals.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and announces</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Subtext is a new medium in which the representation of a program\nis the same thing as its execution.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Subtext is thus a medium or <a href=\"/term/substrate\">substrate</a> in which divergence is minimised.</p>\n<p>However, it seems that a slight pun may emerge between these two notions of “execution” – one, the execution\nof the user’s intention, and the other, the execution of the program. I argue that by reframing the activity of\nsubstrates away from execution in the programming sense, there is better scope for the minimisation of divergence\nto bridge these gulfs, in the typical case where minimal or no computation is required.</p>\n<p>Instead of execution we focus on the correlation or organisation of state, in line with the responsibilities of an\n<a href=\"/term/integration-domain\">integration domain</a>. Computation, where necessary, is expressed in a dialect of\n<a href=\"/term/a-good-function\">good functions</a> in a syntax which will be familiar to users from grade school or spreadsheets.</p>\n","summary":"Defined in my 2016 paper , divergence represents any discrepancy between the runtime state of a program, and the state through which it can be authored. Typical sources of divergence include the program’s call stack , uninterpreted addresses within the heap , event listeners and typical sources of object representation such as vtables and the like. In a tradition of materialised programming, divergence should be minimised, in order to reduce the mental burden on those trying to visualise the effects of their edits.","summaryText":"Defined in my 2016 paper , divergence represents any discrepancy between the runtime state of a program, and the state through which it can be authored. Typical sources of divergence include the program’s call stack , uninterpreted addresses within the heap , event listeners and typical sources of object representation such as vtables and the like. In a tradition of materialised programming, divergence should be minimised, in order to reduce the mental burden on those trying to visualise the effects of their edits.","truncated":true,"prevInSection":{"title":"End user programming","relPermalink":"/term/end-user-programming/"},"nextInSection":{"title":"Disclosable Computing","relPermalink":"/term/disclosable-computing/"}}],"shared":{"lang":"en","menu":[{"name":"Posts","url":"post","weight":1,"children":[]},{"name":"WiP","url":"wip","weight":1,"children":[]},{"name":"About","url":"page/about/","weight":3,"children":[]},{"name":"Docs","identifier":"docs","url":"docs","weight":3,"children":[{"name":"Fluid Signals","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-signals","weight":1,"children":[]},{"name":"Fluid Integration Language","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-integration-language","weight":2,"children":[]},{"name":"Fluid View","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-view","weight":3,"children":[]},{"name":"Fluid PTF","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-ptf","weight":4,"children":[]},{"name":"Fluid tldraw integration","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-tldraw","weight":5,"children":[]},{"name":"Layer Linearisation","parent":"docs","url":"docs/linearisation-algorithm","weight":6,"children":[]}]},{"name":"Communities","url":"communities","weight":4,"children":[]},{"name":"Projects","url":"projects","weight":5,"children":[]},{"name":"Terms","url":"term","weight":5,"children":[]}],"buildYear":2026,"relatedIndex":[{"relPermalink":"/","title":"Antranig Basman","kind":"home","params":{}},{"relPermalink":"/post/","title":"post","kind":"section","params":{}},{"relPermalink":"/wip/","title":"wip","kind":"section","params":{}},{"relPermalink":"/wip/page/2/","title":"wip","kind":"section","params":{}},{"relPermalink":"/docs/","title":"docs","kind":"section","params":{}},{"relPermalink":"/page/","title":"page","kind":"section","params":{}},{"relPe